---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Sky from '../components/Sky.astro';
import '../styles/pages.css';
import { SOUNDS } from '../client/modules/soundscape';
const repo = 'https://github.com/ManasDasri/Flow-study';
const shortcuts = [['S', 'Start or pause the timer'], ['F', 'Enter or leave focus mode'], ['M', 'Mute or unmute your microphone'], ['V', 'Turn your camera on or off'], ['C', 'Open chat and start a message'], ['N', 'Open notifications'], ['?', 'Show the shortcut list']];
---
<Page title="Docs | Flow" bodyClass="plain-page">
    <Sky />
    <header class="plain-nav">
        <a href="/" class="brand" aria-label="Flow home">flow<i></i></a>
        <nav aria-label="Main"><a href="/groups.html">Study groups</a><a href={repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a><a href="/start.html" class="btn" data-home-when-signed-in>Get started</a></nav>
    </header>
    <main class="plain-main docs">
        <h1>How Flow works.</h1>
        <p class="lead">Everything you need to run a study session, on one page.</p>

        <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
            <a href="#start">Getting started</a><a href="#timer">The sun timer</a><a href="#rooms">Rooms and invites</a><a href="#groups">Study groups</a><a href="#sound">Sound</a><a href="#shortcuts">Shortcuts</a><a href="#data">Your data</a><a href="#self-host">Run your own copy</a>
        </nav>

        <section id="start">
            <h2>Getting started</h2>
            <p><a href="/start.html">Setup</a> asks four things: your name, a sky, how long you like to focus, and whether you want background sound. Then you create an account with an email and password. Your choices are saved in your browser and applied to the first room you create.</p>
            <p>After that you land on your home screen. Your name, today’s focus, your streak, and the last seven days are on the left. On the right are the three things you can do: start a room (for people you invite, or open to anyone on the study-groups board), join with a code, or take a seat in a group that’s open now.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="timer">
            <h2>The sun timer</h2>
            <p>The timer is shared: when anyone starts, pauses, or resets it, it changes for everyone in the room. The sun shows progress. It sinks during a focus block and has set when the block ends; during a break it rises again and slowly grows and shrinks as a breathing pace.</p>
            <p>Four focus blocks make a set. After the first three the room moves to a short break, and after the fourth to a long one. The default lengths are 25, 5, and 15 minutes; change them from the settings button under the clock, and the new lengths apply to the whole room. Each block starts by itself when the last one ends, so nobody has to press start; turn off “Start the next block automatically” in the same settings to start each one by hand. The room remembers where its timer is, so a reload or a sleeping laptop picks up the block where it stands.</p>
            <p>Focus mode (the expand button, or <kbd>F</kbd>) hides everything except the sky, the clock, and the people you’re with.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="rooms">
            <h2>Rooms and invites</h2>
            <p>A room has a six-character code. Press Invite to copy a link that carries the code; anyone who opens it signs in and arrives in your room. Type a four-digit PIN before creating a room if you want it locked.</p>
            <p>Calls are encrypted. Video and voice are encrypted in each browser with keys that only the two browsers on a call hold (WebRTC’s DTLS-SRTP), and travel directly between them. When a direct path isn’t possible they pass through a relay, which forwards the encrypted stream and cannot read it. Flow’s own servers never receive your video or voice. A direct call does show the people on it your IP address, as any peer-to-peer call does. In groups from the study-groups board, Flow hides it by sending your calls through the relay instead; you can turn this on or off for any room with “Hide my IP address” in the timer settings. You can turn your camera or microphone off at any time, and Flow works fine with both off. Tasks are shared with the room and stay there after you leave. Chat is not kept as a history: people who join later don’t see earlier messages, and it is gone from your screen when you leave. Start a message with <kbd>/ai</kbd> to ask the study assistant a question.</p>
            <p>The bell in the header collects what happened while you were looking elsewhere: chat messages you hadn’t seen, people joining and leaving, finished focus sessions and breaks, a change of host, and your reminders. Its number shows how many are new, and opening it marks them read; select a chat message there to open the chat. Nothing in it is saved: it clears when you leave or reload the page. When a message arrives while the Chat tab isn’t showing, in focus mode, or while Flow is in another tab, the Chat tab shows an unread count and a short tone plays; opening the tab clears the count. To be told about new messages, reminders, and finished sessions while Flow is in a background tab, tick “Send me system notifications” in the timer settings (the settings button under the clock) and save. Your browser asks for permission the first time, and the choice is kept on this device.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="groups">
            <h2>Study groups</h2>
            <p>The <a href="/groups.html">study-groups board</a> shows two things: rooms that are open right now, and groups planned for later. Anyone can read it; taking a seat or hosting needs an account. Groups hold two to six people.</p>
            <p>Each open group shows its topic and subject, whether cameras are expected and how much talking there is, how many seats are free, and what the room is doing this minute (“Focusing, 14 min left”). Search by topic, filter by subject, or press “Find me a seat” to be placed in the open group with the most room.</p>
            <p>To host, use “Host a group” on the board. Choose “Now” and you land in a new room that is already listed. Choose “Later”, pick a time within the next two weeks, and it appears under “Coming up”, where others can say they’re in. From fifteen minutes before the start, your plan shows “Start now”, which opens the room and replaces the plan with a live listing. You can also list any room without a PIN from its Invite dialog.</p>
            <p>Your room stays on the board while you’re in it and comes off when you leave; if your browser closes unexpectedly, it disappears within two minutes. As host you can stop new people joining from the Invite dialog, and remove someone by hovering their tile and pressing Remove. A removed person cannot rejoin that room. If a listing isn’t about studying, press Report on it: three reports hide a listing.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="sound">
            <h2>Sound</h2>
            <p>The Sound tab has two separate things. Soundscapes are made in your own browser and only you hear them:</p>
            <ul>{SOUNDS.map(([, name, text]) => <li><strong>{name}.</strong> {text}</li>)}</ul>
            <p>Room audio shares a YouTube or Spotify link with everyone. Each person presses play themselves and controls their own volume; playback is not synchronised.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="shortcuts">
            <h2>Shortcuts</h2>
            <dl class="docs-keys">{shortcuts.map(([key, action]) => <div><dt><kbd>{key}</kbd></dt><dd>{action}</dd></div>)}</dl>
            <p>Shortcuts are ignored while you’re typing or a dialog is open. <kbd>C</kbd> and <kbd>N</kbd> leave focus mode to show chat or notifications.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="data">
            <h2>Your data</h2>
            <p>Flow stores your email, your display name, the tasks you add to rooms, and a record of each focus block you finish (when, and how long). If you list or plan a group on the board, its topic and your display name are public, visible without an account, until the listing ends.</p>
            <p>If you sign in with GitHub or Google, the sign-in service also keeps the basic profile they share (your name and a link to your picture); Flow doesn’t use or show it. To see how Flow is used and how fast it loads, the site counts page views and load times with Vercel Web Analytics and Speed Insights. They use no cookies, and page addresses are sent without room codes. Signing up, logging in, and asking for a sign-in email go through Cloudflare Turnstile, a check that tells people from bots; most of the time it asks you nothing. If something breaks in your browser, Flow sends itself a short error report (the message, the line in Flow’s code, and the page, without room codes or anything about you) so it can be fixed. Video and voice are never stored, and calls are encrypted between the browsers taking part. Chat is not shown to anyone who joins later; messages pass through Flow’s database on the way, where they are cleared automatically within three days. A question sent with <kbd>/ai</kbd> goes to the assistant’s provider to be answered and is not kept by Flow. Security details, including what is not protected yet, are in <a href={repo + '/blob/main/SECURITY.md'} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SECURITY.md</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="self-host">
            <h2>Run your own copy</h2>
            <p>Flow is open source under the <a href={repo + '/blob/main/LICENSE'} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MIT license</a>. Clone the <a href={repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">repository</a> and run <code>npm ci</code> then <code>npm run dev:demo</code> to explore it offline with no accounts or keys. The README covers connecting your own Supabase project and deploying, and CONTRIBUTING.md covers sending changes.</p>
        </section>
    </main>
</Page>
